---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { toolPathBySlug } from '@lib/tools/tool-link';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Inflation makes money worth less. Prices rise steadily over time, so a fixed amount of money
      buys less each year. To find the future value of money in today terms, divide the
      amount by (1 + inflation rate) raised to the number of years. For example, at 3% inflation,
      $100,000 will buy what about $47,761 buys today after 25 years.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Try The Inflation Calculator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How It Works <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Inflation compounds, just like interest, but it works against you. Each year prices rise
      by a percentage of the new, higher level, so the cumulative effect over decades is much
      larger than the annual rate suggests. The same force that grows savings through
      compounding shrinks the buying power of idle cash.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tool reports two views: the real value (what your money will buy, in today terms)
      and the future nominal cost (what the same goods will cost in future dollars). They are
      two sides of the same calculation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="examples">
    <h2>Examples <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Purchasing power.</strong> $100,000 at 3% inflation keeps the buying power of
      about $47,761 after 25 years. Nearly half the real value quietly disappears.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Future cost.</strong> The same goods that cost $100,000 today would nominally
      cost about $209,378 in 25 years at 3% inflation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="formula">
    <h2>The Formula <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p><strong>Real value = Amount / (1 + f)^t</strong></p>
    <p>
      where f is the annual inflation rate as a decimal and t is the number of years. The
      future nominal cost flips the division to a multiplication: Amount × (1 + f)^t.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="Real versus nominal">
      <p>Nominal is the face value, the number of dollars. Real is that value adjusted for inflation. A salary can rise in nominal terms yet fall in real terms if prices rise faster.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="ten-year-table">
    <h2>How Much Is ₹1,00,000 Worth After 10 Years? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      It depends on the inflation rate, and the gap between rates grows fast. Here is what
      ₹1,00,000 kept in cash will buy after 10 years, in today's money:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>At 3% inflation:</strong> ₹74,409, a loss of about a quarter.</li>
      <li><strong>At 5% inflation:</strong> ₹61,391, a loss of almost 40%.</li>
      <li><strong>At 7% inflation:</strong> ₹50,835, close to half gone.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Each figure is 1,00,000 ÷ (1 + rate)^10. The calculator works in rupees with Indian digit
      grouping, or in other currencies, so you can run the same check on your own savings.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="real-return">
    <h2>What Is a Real Return? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A real return is what an investment earns after inflation. The exact formula is
      (1 + nominal return) ÷ (1 + inflation) − 1. A fixed deposit paying 7% while inflation runs at
      5% gives 1.07 ÷ 1.05 − 1 = 1.9%, slightly less than the 2% you get by subtracting.
    </p>
    <p>
      The shortcut of subtracting is fine for small rates. However, at high inflation the gap widens,
      and tax on the interest can push a real return below zero even when the nominal number looks
      healthy.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="rule-of-72">
    <h2>How Fast Does Inflation Halve Your Money? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Divide 72 by the inflation rate. At 6% inflation, money loses half its purchasing power in about
      72 ÷ 6 = 12 years. At 4%, it takes about 18 years; at 8%, about 9.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same rule tells you how long an investment takes to double, which is why it works both
      ways. The <a href={toolPathBySlug('rule-of-72-calculator')}>Rule of 72 Calculator</a> compares
      the estimate with the exact answer.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common Mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Confusing real and nominal">
      <p>
        Inflation does not reduce the number of dollars, it reduces what they buy. Mixing up the
        face amount with its purchasing power leads to over optimistic plans for retirement and
        long term goals. Therefore, always check a long term target in today money, not just its
        future dollar figure.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Underestimating the long run">
      <p>
        A 2% to 3% rate sounds harmless, but because it compounds it can halve purchasing power
        over a few decades. Long horizons make even modest inflation a major factor.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="Beating inflation">
      <p>To preserve buying power, your after tax return needs to exceed inflation. Compare an expected investment return against the inflation rate to see whether savings are gaining or losing real ground.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
